Well, the issue, as with everything in NetHack, is
more complex than you could believe. Because, as I
said last time, the tile definitions are part of a
circular transformation set, you can choose to
change the text or the image representation. The
image would probably be easier in terms of drawing,
but would require great care because of the _other_
"gotcha", that there are only the 18 available
colors you listed, assigned to capital letters A
through (probably) R (or S if O gets skipped to
avoid confusion with zero), so you have to do your
editing in some drawing software's indexed mode,
prevent it from anti-aliasing, and lots of similar
hazards. Once the bitmap is changed, though, you can
then use one of the circle of transformation tools
to generate the text source Objects.txt
_automatically_.

Of course, in fixing the menu, you may now have
lunched the use of the standard tiles for floor
mapping instead, if those are indeed the same icon
descriptions the menu uses.

I'm sure I've botched some of the description here,
but that's at least close to what is true.

xanthian.

Were it up to me, I'd cut and paste the existing
transformation code to make a new executable that
lets you work on one icon at a time, and display
it and return the text for it, whether working in
drawing mode or text editing mode, though. The
errors I fixed in the larger 32x32 standard tile set
were all the result of the boundaries between tiles
being invisible in the contiguous *.bmp file, and
the artist, editing on that merged map, drawing
icons that trespassed across the invisible
boundaries. Working on the icons by onesies, and
merging them only when the art was finished,
instead, would avoid that error possibility.

Of course now, The GIMP, and probably other tools,
will happily float a grid "above" the image while
you edit to help you avoid that error working on
the intact *.bmp file, but that is a truth today,
not a historical truth.



